","webcast":false,"url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Babergh Cabinet","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Babergh Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Mid Suffolk ASB service satisfaction dropped sharply due to case backlog and poor communication","lead":"Tenant satisfaction with ASB handling reflects service delivery capability and can indicate stress on housing operations. A documented backlog and communication failure suggests resource or process gaps that affect service quality.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Windows and doors programme delayed by extended manufacturing lead times","lead":"Windows and doors contribute to Decent Homes compliance (thermal comfort, water ingress). Delays to this programme extend the backlog of non-compliant properties and the timeline to meet 2027 remediation deadlines.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Insulation programme significantly behind schedule with 1% completion rate","lead":"The insulation programme is critical to achieving Decent Homes standards by the 2027 deadline. At 1% completion, Babergh is significantly at risk of missing its August 2027 remediation target, which could extend the backlog of non-decent homes.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Mid Suffolk Cabinet","paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","headline":"Mid Suffolk ASB service satisfaction dropped sharply due to case backlog and poor communication","detail":"Mid Suffolk anti-social behaviour satisfaction fell to 46% in Q1 2026 against a 62% target. The council explicitly attributes this to an ASB case backlog where older cases were not updated frequently and had poor communication levels. The team has been working through these cases, and new case handling procedures have been implemented, but satisfaction scores reflect the period of poor service delivery.","category":"governance","matters":"Tenant satisfaction with ASB handling reflects service delivery capability and can indicate stress on housing operations. A documented backlog and communication failure suggests resource or process gaps that affect service quality.","figures":"TSM_TP12_MS: 46% satisfaction Q1 2026 vs 62% target; prior quarter was 61%","quote":"The MSDC ASB satisfaction drop is reflective of the ASB case backlog. Older cases were not updated as frequently or had poor levels of communication.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Mid Suffolk Cabinet","paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","headline":"Windows and doors programme delayed by extended manufacturing lead times","detail":"The programme is currently behind planned delivery timescales due to extended manufacturing lead times affecting installation schedules. However, 41 properties in Babergh and 64 in Mid Suffolk are due for completion in the coming months. The contractor performed strongly in the prior year, and the current pipeline is expected to recover the schedule.","category":"capital","matters":"Windows and doors contribute to Decent Homes compliance (thermal comfort, water ingress). Delays to this programme extend the backlog of non-compliant properties and the timeline to meet 2027 remediation deadlines.","figures":"41 properties in pipeline for Babergh; 64 properties in pipeline for Mid Suffolk","quote":"The programme is currently behind the planned delivery timescales, primarily due to extended manufacturing lead times which have impacted installation schedules.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Mid Suffolk Cabinet","paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","headline":"Insulation programme significantly behind schedule with 1% completion rate","detail":"Synergize (Babergh) experienced a longer-than-planned mobilisation phase and is progressing with only 1% of installations completed as of Q1 2026. Purdy (Mid Suffolk) is further along at 22% but still behind pace. Both contractors are increasing labour resources to accelerate delivery and confirm confidence in achieving programmed targets if property access continues.","category":"capital","matters":"The insulation programme is critical to achieving Decent Homes standards by the 2027 deadline. At 1% completion, Babergh is significantly at risk of missing its August 2027 remediation target, which could extend the backlog of non-decent homes.","figures":"Synergize (Babergh): 1% installations completed; Purdy (Mid Suffolk): 22% installations completed","quote":"Synergize (Babergh) started later within the contract period and experienced a slightly longer mobilisation phase. As a result, progress is currently behind programme, with 1% of installations completed.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Mid Suffolk Cabinet","paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","headline":"Significant backlog of non-decent homes with remediation timeline extending to 2027","detail":"Updated Stock Condition Survey Phase 2 data reveals 545 properties (15.72%) in Babergh and 450 properties (13.23%) in Mid Suffolk do not meet Decent Homes Standard. Primary causes are thermal comfort and roofing defects. At current contractor delivery rates, remediation work is projected to complete by August 2027 for Babergh and September 2027 for Mid Suffolk. The council is seeking to accelerate delivery through additional interim roofing contractors.","category":"capital","matters":"Decent Homes compliance is a statutory requirement. A backlog of nearly 1,000 properties represents substantial asset quality risk and deferred maintenance liability that will require sustained capital investment through 2027.","figures":"BDC: 545 non-decent properties (15.72% of 3,468 stock); MSDC: 450 non-decent properties (13.23% of 3,402 stock). Completion target: BDC August 2027, MSDC September 2027.","quote":"The updated Decent Homes position, incorporating both known and estimated outcomes, is: BDC: 84.28% decent (15.72% non-decent), equating to 545 properties (474 confirmed and 71 estimated) out of 3,468.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Mid Suffolk Cabinet","paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","headline":"Emergency damp and mould hazards not being resolved within 24-hour target","detail":"Category 1 (emergency) damp and mould hazards and inspections are consistently failing the 24-hour resolution target. Babergh achieved 0% on both emergency hazard resolution (0/4) and emergency inspections (0/3). Mid Suffolk achieved 20% and 33% respectively. Root causes include portal system issues and resident scheduling conflicts. This represents complete non-compliance with urgent safety standards.","category":"audit","matters":"Emergency HHSRS Category 1 hazards have statutory 24-hour resolution requirements under Awaab's Law, a key RSH Consumer Standard. Consistent failure indicates a systemic service delivery problem in a safety-critical function.","figures":"Hou_ACD_010B: 0% (0/4 completed); Hou_ACD_010M: 20% (1/5 completed); Hou_ACD_012B: 0% (0/3 completed); Hou_ACD_012M: 33% (1/3 completed). Target: 100%.","quote":"Cat 1 Hazards - 0/4 completed within 24hrs. - 2 Total Mobile Connect portal issues, 1 resident request for appointment after timeframe, 1 no contact","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-08"},"name":"Babergh","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"Mid Suffolk ASB service satisfaction dropped sharply due to case backlog and poor communication","lead":"Tenant satisfaction with ASB handling reflects service delivery capability and can indicate stress on housing operations. A documented backlog and communication failure suggests resource or process gaps that affect service quality.","also":["45 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Mid Suffolk Overview and Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-17","n_findings":46,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"2026-27 Q1 Council Housing Assurance Report Final (002)","top_paper_url":"https://baberghmidsuffolk.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s43565/43565.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":136,"news_drafts_total":3}}